{
  "case_id": "2006-05-380",
  "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "U.S. Solicitor General (General Clement)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress held six hearings over four different Congresses and heard from dozens of witnesses in determining that partial-birth abortions are never medically necessary, pose health risks, and should be banned."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: General Clement, couldn't a similar record be made with respect to what is the more common procedure, the D&E, that involves dismemberment of a fetus inside the womb."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, those doctors testified in the congressional hearings or in the Eighth Circuit or Ninth Circuit or the Second Circuit? There are so many doctors here. Which are the two that you're referring to that do not dilate the cervix fully? Did they testify in any of the district court cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My question would be, if this -- do we owe more deference to a congressional finding or to Congress than we owe to a State legislature? What is -- I mean, I take it a State legislature is democratically elected, and don't we owe similar deference to both?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: General Clement, are not some of the findings by Congress clearly erroneous? For example, there is a statement that no current medical schools provide instruction in the procedure. Now that's clearly wrong, isn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: No. It just wouldn't be a question of the doctor's preference. You would have to refer back to prior cases, and what the prior cases talk about including Stenberg is not that that the doctor simply has a preference, but rather that there has to be a significant body of medical opinion that says that this is safer procedure and necessary for the safety of the mother."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So if they have a different view of the facts, why can't we leave it up to whatever facts develop? If there is an appropriate body of medical opinion that does in fact believe this is necessary for the health of the mother, so be it, and the abortion could be performed and the injunction would say that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Clement",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "GENERAL CLEMENT: Justice Kennedy, what I think I have in mind principally would be a preenforcement challenge that was an as applied challenge. And what I have in mind, you know that's something that there is in other areas of the law, Steffl against Thompson is an example. But what you would have in mind is a doctor who had standing under this Court's abortion jurisprudence would come in and say, look, in my practice I've seen that this procedure would be particularly useful in dealing with preeclampsia or placental previa or some condition."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. SMITH",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SMITH: Well, I think there is, there's been some confusion about the word \"necessary\" and it's been used sometimes to talk about whether there are other procedures that could be used, as opposed to the determination that it is the safest procedure that reduces significantly the risk of very serious complications, not the risks of minor complications."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If we could go back to the first question that the Chief asked you, you said yes, it will prevent abortions because of this uncertain line between the D&X and the D&E. Is there a way that Congress could have written the statute that would have insulated the physician who's performing a D&E?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask you to focus on one particular problem that I think is implicated by"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Ginsburg's question. If I understood you correctly a moment ago, and I think this is in your briefs too, you said that the definitional problem is that doctors always set out to do an intact procedure if they can, because it involves less risk to the mother from, from acts performed inside. And if that's the case, then it would be, I guess in the real world, very difficult for Congress to define a difference between D&E and D&X, because the intention is always, as you understand it, to have an intact result."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Solicitor General",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your brother on the other side, the Solicitor General says there certainly is testimony to the effect that that is not so. That doctors who intend to perform a D&E simply intend at the beginning to have a lesser degree of dilation which will force them to do the D&E and not have a totally intact procedure."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, they may not be able to control it in an absolute sense, but can't they go about it in a way that would tend to produce less rather than more dilation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "show this. I'm not asking you to answer the impossible, but do we have evidence that would indicate that they can or that they can't?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If there were a health exception --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Suppose, this might help, suppose the physician testifies that I wanted to do a non-intact, an in utero D&E, that that's, that was my intent, that's what I wanted to do, that's what I always want to do. In this case I had an intact delivery and had no other choice. Are you saying that we could interpret the statute to say that that is not the prohibited criminal intent, he is immune from prosecution in that case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Smith",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0010",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "there are allegations in the Congressional Record, for example, in reference -- in Justice Thomas' dissent by Nurse Schaffer, Dr. Pamela Smith, about circumstances where the physician actually holds the fetus in the woman's body in order to cause fetal demise, rather than causing fetal demise because it's an integral part of removal of the fetus from the woman's uterus."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Smith",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bbb93ce1dc39-0002-0012",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But in any case, the findings in this case are simply unreasonable and not supported by the evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-05-380",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "bbb93ce1dc3950eab6f481269fa2fb1cb9f1acb9c461ec420037b5df21cbbadb",
